id: E2176
draft: true
title: Task-owned Python dependency and source-closure validation for pure-Python Conan packages
severity: 2
keywords: pure-python, conan, source-closure, pytest, missing-fixture, pythonpath, ohos, target-runtime
symptom: A pure-Python Conan package can complete export, source(), build(), and package() on a minimal Buildbot worker, then fail in test_package because the worker has no third-party Python modules such as scipy or more_itertools.
root_cause: Conan package_info only exposes the packaged module through PYTHONPATH; it does not install the package's external Python runtime dependencies. Importing ConfigSpace eagerly loads SciPy-backed classes and more_itertools helpers even for an empty ConfigurationSpace smoke.
fix: Keep the recipe dependency-neutral when the required Python dependencies are not published as Conan packages. Make the smoke test verify the installed package marker, then explicitly skip the API import when a known external runtime dependency is absent; run real dependency-backed tests separately on a target-owned runtime closure. Never turn a reduced smoke into a dependency-behavior PASS.
verification: Buildbot #37072 reproduced the missing scipy failure after conan create succeeded; #37106 then showed the same worker also lacked numpy. The corrected test now emits an explicit dependency-missing SKIP after validating the package marker, while the target runtime with pinned NumPy, SciPy, pyparsing, typing_extensions, and more_itertools passed 330/330 upstream tests and the consumer smoke.
scope: Pure-Python Conan packages with eager imports of external modules on restricted CI workers or OpenHarmony targets.
safety_boundary: A dependency-missing SKIP validates package installation only, not API, numerical, or dependency behavior. Full dependency-backed upstream tests and target validation remain mandatory; do not fabricate CI, W3, or release evidence from the reduced smoke.
doc_file: drafts/E2176-draft-A7F3.md
